2.8.2 · Map — how a value becomes a color, for every display in the rack including the spectrogram.
2 and 3 mix the START, MID and END swatches;
HUE is a raw hue sweep;
heat, blue, gray, turbo, viridis and magma are the published colormaps.
Turbo and viridis are the even ones — equal steps in the value look like equal steps in color, which a hue sweep does not give you.
This is the only colormap control there is: the spectrogram had a second one naming the same six maps, and two knobs that had to be kept in step by hand were one knob too many.

title:
3.6 · Distortion — THD, THD+N, SINAD and ENOB of the live audio, measured against whichever tone is in it.
Feed it a generator's sine at 1 kHz (type 1000 into its Hz readout) through whatever you want measured and read how much of what comes back is not that tone.
THD is the harmonics alone, which is the number a specification quotes;
THD+N is everything that is not the fundamental, which is the honest one and always the larger;
SINAD is the same ratio in decibels, the way a converter is specified;
ENOB runs the ideal-converter relation backwards and says how many bits would sound this clean.
The gap between THD and THD+N is how much of the rubbish is hiss rather than distortion.
The instrument's own floor is about 0.0015% THD+N (96.5 dB SINAD, 17.3 effective bits), measured on a synthesized pure tone — that is the Blackman-Harris window's leakage past the notch, and nothing quieter than it can be read.

controls.title:
4.2.2 · Trigger — LEVEL is the value the signal must cross, in the SLOPE direction, for a sweep to start;
set it inside the waveform or nothing triggers.
SLOPE, the buttons at the right: + triggers on the signal rising through the level, − on it falling.
MODE, the buttons at the left: AUTO sweeps anyway when nothing crosses, so silence shows a baseline;
NORM sweeps only on a trigger and holds the last trace otherwise.

title:
4.3 · Gen 1 — a generator, for sound or for modulation.
The FREQ knob, an encoder with no stops — a click a semitone, a turn an octave, the ring's color which octave — from 0.107 Hz to 28160 Hz, a one-octave piano strip under it lighting the note it is on;
the level knob inside its envelope's ATTACK and DECAY rings, with ENV between them to repeat it;
and the waveform knob, OFF at the bottom, with SOLO beside it to hear only this one and OUT under it.

Where it goes is OUT: OFF, L, C (both sides) or R, on the speakers and the rack's signal together.
The Mixer's GEN 1 column has every other pinning — the speakers or the rack alone, a gain, an inverted side, the sends to modulation — and OUT reads MIX while it does.

